WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.500
Flatline. Movies love this.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.240
Whenever there's a flatline,
they like shocking it.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.328
It's essentially like trying to

00:00:10.328 --> 00:00:11.753
jump-start a car with no battery.

00:00:11.753 --> 00:00:12.630
Hi, my name's Randy Li.

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:14.130
I'm a paramedic here in New York City.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:15.900
I've been doing this for about 11 years,

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.220
and my role as a paramedic

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:19.650
is to provide prehospital
care to patients.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:21.930
Today, we're going to be
looking at medical emergencies

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:25.008
from movies and TV and judge
how realistic they are.

00:00:27.577 --> 00:00:29.460
[Stuart chocking]

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:30.476
Stu.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:32.610
Oh, he's choking.

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:35.220
The character is choking
on a shrimp, apparently.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.290
He never did the universal choking sign.

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:39.360
He definitely stopped
making any kind of noise,

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.280
so that's definitely a sign.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:42.960
People who are like, "Ah,
I'm choking, I'm choking."

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.310
Or they say, and they kind of cough,

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:47.768
or they're making this audible
noise, and you're like,

00:00:47.768 --> 00:00:49.530
"Well, you're not really
choking. Air's moving."

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:51.210
No sound was coming out of his airway.

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:53.400
So you can tell that there
was no passage of air

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:54.369
one way or the other.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:59.585
Mrs. Doubtfire runs over,
begins doing abdominal thrusts.

00:00:59.585 --> 00:01:02.640
In my protocols, that's
exactly what you would do.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:03.990
That position is correct.

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:06.510
The hand position is
just above the umbilicus,

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:07.500
where your belly button is.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.150
I couldn't see perfectly well

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:11.790
because obviously Pierce Brosnan's
character has clothes on.

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:13.770
But if you're right
around the belly button,

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:14.610
just a little bit higher,

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:15.720
that's where you're supposed to be.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.880
Then do a J motion upward,

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:18.990
which pretty much would look like

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:20.760
what Mrs. Doubtfire was doing.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:22.170
Depending what system you work,

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:23.280
there are five back blows

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:24.750
that are associated with it as well,

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.540
before they do the Heimlich
or abdominal thrust.

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:30.240
The five back blows is not
something we deploy here,

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:31.440
not in New York City.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.240
And none of it's considerably wrong,

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.100
it just happens to be
what works in your system

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.470
for what patients you encounter.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.829
Come on! Come on!

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:45.259
Lifting, that's wrong.

00:01:45.259 --> 00:01:46.920
Even if the patient was very, very small

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:49.170
and you could easily lift
somebody up like that,

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:50.970
I just don't see where
the benefits would be.

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:53.700
This is already considered
a fairly dangerous procedure

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:56.003
because you can, you know, break ribs.

00:01:56.003 --> 00:01:59.280
I think there were some
cracking sounds in there,

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:00.720
so maybe he broke his back.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.480
Abdominal thrusts, back slaps

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:06.030
is considered a first-aid treatment.

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:08.340
I think it's a fair enough example,

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:11.250
but you're best off to
actually take a proper class

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.815
and not reference something
from TV or a movie.

00:02:13.815 --> 00:02:15.000
I gotta give it a 5.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.710
I like it. It's Robin Williams.

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:17.880
Find her heart.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:18.840
I'm giving her a shot in the heart,

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:20.070
so I guess it's gotta be f---ing exact.

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:21.270
I don't know exactly where her heart is.

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:23.430
I mean, I think it's right here.

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:24.420
Come on, man, hurry up!

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:25.890
Oh, I know this scene.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:27.930
That's just wrong.

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:29.520
Adrenaline's also known as epinephrine.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:30.720
That's not the first line of treatment.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:35.070
The medication is fair if the
patient was in cardiac arrest.

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:37.680
This is a heroin overdose, and
that's definitely not right.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.660
We would give something
called Narcan. Naloxone.

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:41.700
That can actually be delivered
three different ways.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:42.992
From a civilian standpoint,

00:02:42.992 --> 00:02:44.741
they actually do have these kits.

00:02:44.741 --> 00:02:47.130
You can give that intranasally,
so right through the nose;

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:49.590
intramuscularly, through
the muscle essentially,

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:51.282
like they arm; and then IV.

00:02:51.282 --> 00:02:53.515
You wouldn't have to necessarily
stab it through the chest.

00:02:55.207 --> 00:02:56.748
All right. All right, tell me what to do.

00:02:56.748 --> 00:02:58.470
I've never marked anyone's
chest with a marker.

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:00.120
That's weird. OK.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.980
The marking is actually pretty fair

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:03.240
in relation to where the heart is.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:04.170
There's this misconception

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:05.430
that it's, like, all the
way over to the left.

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:08.640
So if you're doing Pledge of
Allegiance, they go this far,

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:09.690
like this is where the heart is.

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:10.890
It's actually about right here,

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.507
just slightly over to the left.

00:03:12.507 --> 00:03:13.790
Ready? One.

00:03:15.865 --> 00:03:16.698
[gasps]

00:03:16.698 --> 00:03:17.850
They always like doing this stuff.

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:20.225
It's great for film.
It looks super dynamic.

00:03:20.225 --> 00:03:22.620
I can't imagine ever taking a medication,

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:24.600
just, pop, stabbing him in the chest.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:25.650
It's not how it works.

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:27.660
A variety of things that can go wrong.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:30.030
When we deliver medication,
it's to the bloodstream,

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:31.140
so it circulates the body.

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:33.810
Now, if you just hit the heart,

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:36.030
then it disperses in a
completely different way,

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:37.440
and you might miss.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.900
I mean, there's bones,
there's a sternum in the way,

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.270
there's floating ribs,
and ribs in general.

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:44.970
There are ways to do something like that

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:46.147
where we establish something called

00:03:46.147 --> 00:03:48.120
intraosseous access in the chest.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:50.640
That's basically drilling a needle

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:53.070
into the chest plate, into
the sternum specifically.

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:55.500
And then that gives us
access to give medication.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:58.699
To stab it through bone like
that person's suggesting,

00:03:58.699 --> 00:03:59.911
that doesn't happen.

00:03:59.911 --> 00:04:02.578
[Mia yelling]

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:06.188
I've never treated anyone that just

00:04:06.188 --> 00:04:07.530
straight popped up like that.

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:09.960
In terms of an epi thing
or adrenaline thing,

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:13.650
they generally kind of just
breathe a little bit faster

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:15.720
and their heart rate increases.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.900
But with this being that
it's a heroin overdose

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:21.840
and then you give Narcan,
it's a slow response.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.030
And you'll have some
respiratory responses first,

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:26.400
then consciousness should come back

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:27.570
if they were knocked out.

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:29.310
It's not like drinking 20 energy drinks,

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:30.510
where you just pop up.

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:31.890
I'll give it a 2,

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:34.890
because they said the
heart was on the left

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:36.720
and the rest was weird.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.120
Resnick and Dr. Farcy,
we're trauma surgeons.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:40.200
Show us what you got.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:43.350
You can see the abdominal
area's really swollen.

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:44.520
I'm thinking there's internal bleeding.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:45.870
The telemedicine part's cool.

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:48.750
My system is starting to
utilize that kind of thing.

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:52.050
It could be something as simple
as a cellphone or a tablet,

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:53.790
not a tablet like hers,
that was really fancy.

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:56.280
All systems currently have a system

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:57.870
by which you can
communicate with a doctor.

00:04:57.870 --> 00:05:01.530
However, the doctor's usually
specifically on call for that.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:03.997
They wouldn't necessarily
be on the golf course.

00:05:03.997 --> 00:05:05.550
"Oh, look, trauma's coming in."

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:08.495
No, they have to be dedicated
for that specific thing.

00:05:08.495 --> 00:05:10.484
I'm going for the skin.

00:05:10.484 --> 00:05:11.900
OK, I'm cutting.
Randy: What?

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:13.680
She uses shears like these.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:16.710
We use this specifically to cut clothes.

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:19.693
At no point have I ever used this to cut

00:05:19.693 --> 00:05:22.309
a body part or skin.

00:05:22.309 --> 00:05:24.238
Also, it's actually ill-advised

00:05:24.238 --> 00:05:26.700
to ever cut off any kind of flap of skin.

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:28.740
Let's suppose maybe
there was an amputation.

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:30.600
At no point are you
supposed to cut that off

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:32.100
to, like, get it out of the way.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:34.842
I need you to blunt dissect
with your hand, right?

00:05:34.842 --> 00:05:36.600
Mm-mm. Mm-mm. Negative.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:37.741
No, no.

00:05:37.741 --> 00:05:39.999
We don't do surgeries
in moving ambulances,

00:05:39.999 --> 00:05:42.000
or not-moving ambulances as well.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:42.990
It's not a sterile space.

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:44.490
It's probably pretty dirty, honestly.

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:46.590
If you're going to be opening up somebody,

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:48.960
if you're not treating with antibiotics,

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:50.070
you might save them for the moment,

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:51.360
but then they'll die of an infection.

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:54.180
But we will take care of gunshot victims,

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:56.400
and depending on your system,

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:57.990
the rules change slightly differently.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:01.320
In my system currently,
we administer a medication

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:03.300
called TXA, tranexamic acid,

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:05.310
that basically forces blood to coagulate.

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:07.800
So it can kind of hinder
the bleeding process.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:09.960
But there's not really much
we can do outside of that.

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:12.330
What our main job is, to
get them to the hospital,

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:14.106
where they can actually do this safely.

00:06:14.106 --> 00:06:15.637
Slow down, man!

00:06:15.637 --> 00:06:18.480
We have a live operation
back here, just slow down!

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:20.130
Sure, that's a problem.

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:22.230
There's always this old trope for EMS,

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:23.610
you know, doing the same
thing the hospital does

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:24.870
but at 60 miles an hour.

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:27.090
And that's true, we do do that sometimes.

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:27.960
We're always taught to drive

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:29.340
with something called due regard.

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:31.770
We are allowed to speed a little bit,

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:34.350
we're allowed to, like,
go past red lights,

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:35.970
but you have to do it in a
really, really smart way.

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:37.110
You have to be safe about it.

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:38.700
I've just straight-up
yelled at the driver,

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:40.920
"Stop, count to 10, then go again."

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:42.673
For those 10 seconds, I have some time

00:06:42.673 --> 00:06:46.110
to do my IVs and whatever I need to do.

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:49.685
I give them points for
having telemedicine.

00:06:49.685 --> 00:06:50.518
2?

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:55.270
I am so sorry.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:58.380
Yeah, that's called murder.

00:06:58.380 --> 00:06:59.730
He stabbed her!

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:01.650
No paramedic out there
is going to stab someone.

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:03.870
I'm assuming it was something
like a tension pneumothorax

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:05.280
where they had to release the air.

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:07.560
That basically just means
that you have air trapping

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:10.320
in your lungs and it's
expanding, expanding, expanding.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:13.020
And this is the one time
you'll ever hear about

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:15.600
a paramedic stabbing someone
in the chest, essentially.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:17.130
But it's with a really specific tool.

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:18.556
If you're using something like this,

00:07:18.556 --> 00:07:20.010
this is a decompression needle,

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:22.050
so that's the needle
that you would utilize.

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:24.330
So, you would find your landmark.

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:25.710
There are essentially two locations.

00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:29.490
One's down the midclavicular
line, so mid-clavicle.

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:31.740
And there's another spot
down the midaxillary line,

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:33.240
so on the side over here.

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:35.850
At that point, you would then
remove the needle itself,

00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:38.130
and then this plastic
piece would stay there.

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:40.680
That's how you release
the air from the lungs.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:43.680
Using a knife to do it doesn't work

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:46.710
because you're essentially
going to be cutting the lung.

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:48.843
The lung won't have the
ability to reinflate again.

00:07:48.843 --> 00:07:53.551
[Furiosa wheezing]

00:07:53.551 --> 00:07:55.080
Why is she making that noise?

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:56.010
In terms of the wheezing,

00:07:56.010 --> 00:07:58.350
I'm pretty sure that's just done for TV

00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:00.870
to make it sound like there's
some kind of breathing issue.

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:02.400
Wheezing itself is usually associated

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:04.500
with something like asthma or anaphylaxis,

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:05.670
like an allergic reaction.

00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:07.740
It's just not the symptom or sign

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:09.630
that will come along with
a tension pneumothorax.

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:11.130
If you were listening to lung sounds

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:12.197
for someone with a tension pneumo,

00:08:12.197 --> 00:08:14.340
you would notice that there's
diminished lung sounds

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:15.498
on the affected side.

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:23.130
What kind of needle is that?

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:24.330
It looks like a bone.

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:26.820
It doesn't look like the needle was clean.

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:30.511
So, this is an actual IV catheter.

00:08:30.511 --> 00:08:35.340
It's in a sealed bag. So this is clean.

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:38.190
I can open this right now
and use it on a patient.

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:40.140
There's also something we apply,

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:41.490
some people call it a tourniquet,

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:43.560
we call it a venous-constricting band.

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:45.823
It'll allow your veins to kind
of pop up a little bit more

00:08:45.823 --> 00:08:48.030
so you can make sure you
actually hit the vein.

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:49.980
Because that's specifically
why you're doing IVs.

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:53.160
He took a needle that
kind of looked like a bone

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:54.840
and proceeded to poke himself,

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:57.750
and then proceeded to poke
Charlize Theron's character.

00:08:57.750 --> 00:08:59.340
I, personally, as a
paramedic here in New York,

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:01.380
do not do blood transfusions in the field.

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:02.430
I gotta give it, like, a 1.

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:04.110
As paramedics, we don't hurt our patients,

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:05.760
so the fact that he stabbed her,

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:07.835
you get sent straight to the bottom.

00:09:07.835 --> 00:09:08.869
Palmer: What happened?

00:09:08.869 --> 00:09:11.869
Strange: Stabbed. Cardiac tamponade.

00:09:12.819 --> 00:09:14.022
Yay, Marvel stuff.

00:09:14.022 --> 00:09:17.010
Dr. Strange walks in, and he
says, "I'm cardiac tamponade."

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:20.067
Basically, that means you have
blood surrounding the heart,

00:09:20.067 --> 00:09:22.770
and it's restricting the
heart from actually pumping.

00:09:22.770 --> 00:09:24.990
How he knows that is beyond me.

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:26.760
Quite honestly, this is
one of those situations

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:28.620
where we really can't do that much.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:30.733
The big benefit of us showing up would be,

00:09:30.733 --> 00:09:32.640
through our assessments,
figure out what it is.

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:34.350
Then we have the ability
to go to the hospital,

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:35.640
give the notification early.

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:37.320
Then hopefully that means

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:38.850
they don't have to spend extra time

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:40.175
reassessing the patient again.

00:09:44.343 --> 00:09:45.810
Chest cavity's clear.

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:47.677
Oh, percussion. OK, cool.

00:09:47.677 --> 00:09:51.900
So, percussing is an actual
way to do an assessment.

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:53.430
We're trained in it, to a certain degree,

00:09:53.430 --> 00:09:57.030
but it's not the most effective
thing, in my experience.

00:09:57.030 --> 00:09:59.520
Basically, you're checking
for, like, hyperresonance,

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:00.870
or basically just the way it sounds.

00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:04.980
If you thump somebody in
the chest and there's fluid,

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:07.230
then it would be a lot
more solid-sounding.

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:08.790
Hyperresonance would
mean that it's hollow,

00:10:08.790 --> 00:10:09.810
and it sounds a little bit different.

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:12.000
I would probably just take
my stethoscope and listen in,

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:14.970
and listen to see if the heartbeat sounds

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:17.328
are a little more muffled versus not.

00:10:22.710 --> 00:10:25.830
She is performing a pericardiocentesis.

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:27.660
She did the right procedure,

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:30.457
taking the needle, putting
it through, essentially,

00:10:30.457 --> 00:10:32.670
the chest to get to the
heart to drain out the fluid.

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:34.200
I don't personally know how to do that,

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:35.340
because that's out of
my scope of practice.

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:36.570
I just know what it is.

00:10:36.570 --> 00:10:38.790
I would imagine, though,
you would normally use

00:10:38.790 --> 00:10:40.410
some way of scanning, maybe an ultrasound,

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:42.030
so you could see where
the layer of the heart is

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:44.790
versus the little membrane on the outside

00:10:44.790 --> 00:10:46.350
where the blood might
have been accumulating.

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:48.180
So this is why it's a doctoral procedure,

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:49.410
not a paramedic procedure.

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:51.330
While we do have ultrasounds
on the ambulance,

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:52.410
depending where you work,

00:10:52.410 --> 00:10:54.602
in our space it's just too
dangerous. Things move.

00:10:56.850 --> 00:10:58.553
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah,
yeah. Flatline, good.

00:10:58.553 --> 00:11:00.150
Let's shock this. This is not a thing.

00:11:00.150 --> 00:11:02.040
Flatline, movies love this.

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:03.720
Whenever there's a flatline,
they like shocking it.

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:06.810
The whole point of
defibrillation, shocking a person,

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:09.510
is to stop V-tach or V-fib,

00:11:09.510 --> 00:11:11.760
where the electrical
conductivity of the heart,

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:13.410
it's going all over the place.

00:11:13.410 --> 00:11:15.240
So you defibrillate it

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:18.090
to allow the heart's
automaticity to take over,

00:11:18.090 --> 00:11:21.150
to basically reset it back
into a regular rhythm.

00:11:21.150 --> 00:11:23.670
When there is no rhythm,
there's nothing to shock.

00:11:23.670 --> 00:11:24.767
It's essentially like trying to

00:11:24.767 --> 00:11:26.183
jump-start a car with no battery.

00:11:26.183 --> 00:11:28.500
Also, the paddles, great
for TV. Super cool-looking.

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:30.840
I'm sure there are still hospitals
out there that have them,

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:33.840
but it's not great for us as
prehospital-care providers

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:35.947
because there was a very
specific way you had to do it.

00:11:35.947 --> 00:11:37.480
You had to put the gel on, you
had to put it on the chest,

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:39.570
you had to do, like, 15 pounds of pressure

00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:42.300
to make sure it was even,
and then, pff, shock them.

00:11:42.300 --> 00:11:44.700
We have the actual electrodes
that just go on there.

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:46.620
They're safer, more efficient.

00:11:46.620 --> 00:11:48.660
I kind of want to give everything 2s.

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:50.247
But I gotta give it a 2. I gotta do it.

00:11:51.489 --> 00:11:52.650
I didn't know what to do.

00:11:52.650 --> 00:11:53.790
The bike's crushing the guy,

00:11:53.790 --> 00:11:55.770
but I didn't think I should move him.

00:11:55.770 --> 00:11:57.055
You tell me.

00:11:58.770 --> 00:12:00.480
I personally have gone
to motorcycle accidents

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:02.280
where very similar to this have happened.

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:03.960
PD does show up first sometimes.

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:04.890
In this kind of situation,

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:06.420
I'm pretty sure they
would've done the same thing

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:07.950
this police officer did.

00:12:07.950 --> 00:12:10.860
They would've held off, because
it's considered a procedure.

00:12:10.860 --> 00:12:12.510
You don't know what's
happening underneath,

00:12:12.510 --> 00:12:14.530
because what if maybe this motorcycle's

00:12:14.530 --> 00:12:16.146
sitting on top of an injury

00:12:16.146 --> 00:12:17.946
and restricting it from bleeding more?

00:12:17.946 --> 00:12:19.260
So they know we're coming

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:20.790
and then we'll just take care of it.

00:12:20.790 --> 00:12:21.810
On three.

00:12:21.810 --> 00:12:23.253
One, two, three.

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:28.560
In terms of removing it, the
motorcycle, it seemed fair.

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:29.813
When they removed it,
they noticed there were

00:12:29.813 --> 00:12:31.546
other injuries, and obviously that's why

00:12:31.546 --> 00:12:33.687
you remove the motorcycle,
to basically give you access

00:12:33.687 --> 00:12:35.217
to the patient to do assessments.

00:12:35.217 --> 00:12:36.930
That's the only thing
that's a little bit sketchy,

00:12:36.930 --> 00:12:38.940
is the fact that they
removed it from the patient,

00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:40.380
they went over his head.

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:41.550
You wouldn't do that.

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:43.770
You would probably go
towards the feet or away

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:45.210
just because there have been situations

00:12:45.210 --> 00:12:48.420
where vehicles and
things had to be elevated

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:49.650
so we can remove a patient out,

00:12:49.650 --> 00:12:51.270
and unfortunately there have been times

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:53.550
when that elevation process failed

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:55.170
and it went right back down.

00:12:55.170 --> 00:12:57.390
You would generally try
to avoid any kind of area

00:12:57.390 --> 00:13:00.690
like the face, the head, just
in case there is a mishap.

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:06.030
Popping on a tourniquet, OK.

00:13:06.030 --> 00:13:08.250
Tourniquets are utilized to basically stop

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:10.530
any kind of arterial bleed, big bleeds.

00:13:10.530 --> 00:13:12.480
That didn't look like
an arterial bleed to me.

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:14.040
He could have been staging,

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:16.440
meaning you put the
tourniquet in the right place

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:18.630
but not tighten it all the way

00:13:18.630 --> 00:13:20.670
just in case something
happens down the line.

00:13:20.670 --> 00:13:22.080
But yeah, that was OK.

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:25.650
It looks like the leg that was
tourniqueted was also broken.

00:13:25.650 --> 00:13:26.730
It must have been visually broken

00:13:26.730 --> 00:13:28.980
for them to automatically
start putting a splint on it.

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:30.066
So that's fair treatment.

00:13:30.066 --> 00:13:31.722
I'm going to give him air.

00:13:31.722 --> 00:13:34.955
See? Shears being used for shear purposes.

00:13:34.955 --> 00:13:36.360
Oh, my God, you're not
holding stabilization,

00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:37.230
you're killing me.

00:13:37.230 --> 00:13:39.960
That's weird, but not terrible.

00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:42.480
Removing of the helmet, that was good.

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:44.580
Techniques change from person to person

00:13:44.580 --> 00:13:46.020
on how they like to do it,

00:13:46.020 --> 00:13:48.450
but I think that that
was overall pretty fair.

00:13:48.450 --> 00:13:49.830
They kind of flubbed a little bit

00:13:49.830 --> 00:13:52.470
in terms of holding the
C-spine, holding the neck,

00:13:52.470 --> 00:13:54.060
so it didn't kind of jostle.

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:56.340
They were doing, like, holding
from the back of the head,

00:13:56.340 --> 00:13:58.020
to not twist left or right.

00:13:58.020 --> 00:13:59.400
Basically hold it in place like that.

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:02.880
Normally when I do it, I
hold the side of the head,

00:14:02.880 --> 00:14:06.090
like ear-to-ear kind of
area, and hold it like that.

00:14:06.090 --> 00:14:08.970
But that could just be
positioning, where they were.

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:11.195
That's essentially a
little bit forgivable.

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:16.400
Don't move. It's OK.

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:20.520
They did put on the C-spine collar,

00:14:20.520 --> 00:14:22.050
the cervical-spine collar, correctly.

00:14:22.050 --> 00:14:22.950
It was a fair height.

00:14:22.950 --> 00:14:25.091
You generally want it where

00:14:25.091 --> 00:14:26.499
not that the head's too high up

00:14:26.499 --> 00:14:28.560
and not that they have
this ability to look down

00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:29.940
and kind of swish their head around.

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:31.110
Because if there are spinal injuries,

00:14:31.110 --> 00:14:33.510
you don't want them twisting
around and causing more damage.

00:14:33.510 --> 00:14:35.040
That's something I would do, 100%.

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:37.230
Little flubs here and there,
but the truth of the matter

00:14:37.230 --> 00:14:38.970
is when we're working
in these environments

00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:40.050
and doing these kind of scenes,

00:14:40.050 --> 00:14:42.210
stuff may not go perfectly every time.

00:14:42.210 --> 00:14:44.213
It's a 9. Easy 9.

00:14:44.213 --> 00:14:47.131
[man panting]

00:14:47.131 --> 00:14:48.186
Breathe.

00:14:48.186 --> 00:14:51.369
One, two, three, four, breathe!

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:54.540
I think that the patient's
in cardiac arrest.

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:56.790
So you would continually do CPR,

00:14:56.790 --> 00:14:58.290
but that technique is terrible.

00:14:58.290 --> 00:15:00.213
His CPR was super inconsistent.

00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:03.390
He did five, then he stopped,

00:15:03.390 --> 00:15:05.550
and maybe he did a couple
more, then he stopped.

00:15:05.550 --> 00:15:09.240
There's this threshold of
compressions you have to maintain.

00:15:09.240 --> 00:15:11.820
You're squishing, essentially, the heart

00:15:11.820 --> 00:15:12.840
to pump blood through it.

00:15:12.840 --> 00:15:14.977
You're supposed to do it
about 100 beats per minute.

00:15:14.977 --> 00:15:16.455
"The Abyss" was made back in the '80s,

00:15:16.455 --> 00:15:20.880
so protocol for CPR ratios
have changed since then.

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:22.320
How we practice it now,

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:23.930
you're supposed to do 30 compressions

00:15:23.930 --> 00:15:26.220
on an adult patient in cardiac arrest.

00:15:26.220 --> 00:15:28.170
And then you can have
that little threshold

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:30.170
where you can stop to give ventilations.

00:15:37.380 --> 00:15:39.240
So, that's questionable.

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:41.280
I just don't see the point
of doing something like that.

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:43.890
Using a bag-valve mask is
always going to be better

00:15:43.890 --> 00:15:45.090
than going mouth-to-mouth,

00:15:45.090 --> 00:15:47.400
which will allow for more
oxygen to be delivered

00:15:47.400 --> 00:15:48.540
versus mouth-to-mouth.

00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:52.380
Ambient air has 21% oxygen.
You exhale out 16% oxygen.

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:54.330
And then with the BVM, the bag-valve mask,

00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:56.220
you can actually connect it to oxygen

00:15:56.220 --> 00:15:58.590
and then deliver nearly
100% oxygen to the patient.

00:15:58.590 --> 00:16:00.420
But if you're a layperson

00:16:00.420 --> 00:16:02.100
and you're trying to do CPR on someone,

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:04.740
then hands-only CPR is a thing.

00:16:04.740 --> 00:16:06.150
Now, other systems, they may not do that.

00:16:06.150 --> 00:16:07.980
Maybe they still encourage mouth-to-mouth.

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:10.200
But according to the
American Heart Association,

00:16:10.200 --> 00:16:12.450
we do hands-only CPR.

00:16:12.450 --> 00:16:14.310
There's enough oxygen in
the blood to circulate

00:16:14.310 --> 00:16:17.393
to buy you some time for
advanced life-care support.

00:16:19.302 --> 00:16:20.287
- Come on, baby.
- Clear.

00:16:20.287 --> 00:16:21.120
Come on, baby.

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:21.953
Clear!

00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:25.770
You would definitely
do the defibrillation,

00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:28.110
but I don't know how they're
looking at the heart.

00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:30.090
There's no electrodes on her,

00:16:30.090 --> 00:16:32.700
they're not representing any kind of EKG

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:34.650
that obviously reads
electrical data of your heart.

00:16:34.650 --> 00:16:38.093
So could've been another
flatliner who got shot?

00:16:38.093 --> 00:16:38.926
Maybe.

00:16:38.926 --> 00:16:40.830
You've never backed away
from anything in your life,

00:16:40.830 --> 00:16:42.000
now fight!

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:44.053
Fight, fight!

00:16:44.053 --> 00:16:47.220
Oh, my God. He just slapped her!

00:16:47.220 --> 00:16:48.870
I've never just straight slapped somebody

00:16:48.870 --> 00:16:51.570
and had them wake up
from a cardiac arrest.

00:16:51.570 --> 00:16:54.360
If you take it from the
value of what they're doing

00:16:54.360 --> 00:16:57.060
and not how they're
doing it, not the worst.

00:16:57.060 --> 00:16:59.217
I'm going to give it a 3.

00:16:59.217 --> 00:17:01.764
LAFD. Open up, please.

00:17:01.764 --> 00:17:04.350
This is not OK!

00:17:04.350 --> 00:17:05.333
Oh, it's a Smurf.

00:17:05.333 --> 00:17:07.290
I've seen people that kind of blue too,

00:17:07.290 --> 00:17:10.050
but usually it's more like
the tips of their fingers

00:17:10.050 --> 00:17:12.840
or lips, around the eyes and the face,

00:17:12.840 --> 00:17:14.880
because you have a little bit of cyanosis,

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:16.470
less oxygen perfusion.

00:17:16.470 --> 00:17:18.240
You just don't have enough
oxygen going through your body,

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:19.530
so you look blue.

00:17:19.530 --> 00:17:21.792
Have you taken any colloidal
silver supplements?

00:17:21.792 --> 00:17:23.220
What? No.

00:17:23.220 --> 00:17:26.250
So, they first asked
about colloidal silvers.

00:17:26.250 --> 00:17:30.630
So, there are these kind
of holistic medications

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:32.473
where they actually literally have

00:17:32.473 --> 00:17:34.056
flakes of silver in liquid,

00:17:34.056 --> 00:17:35.456
but it can negatively affect you

00:17:35.456 --> 00:17:37.140
and give you this blue skin tone.

00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:39.210
I think it's argyria, that's the effect.

00:17:39.210 --> 00:17:40.740
That's why you saw them initially asking,

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:42.180
"Are you taking this silver supplement?"

00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:43.830
Because they're worried about argyria.

00:17:43.830 --> 00:17:45.930
Any medications we should know about?

00:17:45.930 --> 00:17:48.814
Benzocaine, toothache gel.
She used the whole thing.

00:17:48.814 --> 00:17:50.051
She's seizing.

00:17:50.051 --> 00:17:51.172
I got her.

00:17:52.189 --> 00:17:53.670
It could be hemoglobin anemia.

00:17:53.670 --> 00:17:56.070
To assess a patient so quickly,

00:17:56.070 --> 00:17:57.150
they did a really great job.

00:17:57.150 --> 00:17:58.741
So, whenever we do scenarios

00:17:58.741 --> 00:18:00.699
in EMS school, paramedic school,

00:18:00.699 --> 00:18:02.100
we use scenarios where it's like,

00:18:02.100 --> 00:18:04.080
OK, you associate one thing with another.

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:05.790
That's how you do your assessment so fast.

00:18:05.790 --> 00:18:07.590
When the paramedic comes
out with the benzocaine,

00:18:07.590 --> 00:18:09.725
benzocaine is a medication to kind of help

00:18:09.725 --> 00:18:12.210
alleviate pain from a
toothache, essentially.

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:15.322
Automatically, any
paramedic who knows about

00:18:15.322 --> 00:18:16.440
this disease process,

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:19.230
a very large amount of, like, benzocaine,

00:18:19.230 --> 00:18:21.270
you potentially put yourself

00:18:21.270 --> 00:18:23.370
in something called methemoglobinemia.

00:18:23.370 --> 00:18:25.080
Basically there's this other binding agent

00:18:25.080 --> 00:18:27.000
on top of your red blood cells

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:29.730
that inhibits it from
carrying oxygen anymore.

00:18:29.730 --> 00:18:31.980
In terms of having a seizure
with methemoglobinemia,

00:18:31.980 --> 00:18:34.890
then that's totally valid,
that can totally happen.

00:18:34.890 --> 00:18:36.360
I think that's exactly
what I would've done.

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:38.100
A patient's seizing and
they're sitting in a chair,

00:18:38.100 --> 00:18:40.208
put them to the floor, because
you don't want them to flop

00:18:40.208 --> 00:18:42.664
and then hit the floor.
They could hurt themselves.

00:18:42.664 --> 00:18:44.626
I'll grab the methylene blue chloride.

00:18:44.626 --> 00:18:46.192
It's the benzocaine, what we use

00:18:46.192 --> 00:18:48.600
to prevent the body from
releasing red blood cells.

00:18:48.600 --> 00:18:51.390
They deployed, administered,
methylene blue,

00:18:51.390 --> 00:18:52.650
which is a real medication.

00:18:52.650 --> 00:18:54.150
You deliver it just for this purpose.

00:18:54.150 --> 00:18:58.410
It basically turns the blood
more receptive to oxygen.

00:18:58.410 --> 00:18:59.820
I think it was actually really good.

00:18:59.820 --> 00:19:01.680
I like that they explored multiple options

00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:03.840
before they got to what it was.

00:19:03.840 --> 00:19:05.910
I'll give that a solid 9.

00:19:05.910 --> 00:19:09.300
My favorite medical-emergency TV show

00:19:09.300 --> 00:19:11.490
was a show that was
short-lived called "Sirens."

00:19:11.490 --> 00:19:13.050
It was hysterical.

00:19:13.050 --> 00:19:13.995
Thanks for watching.

00:19:13.995 --> 00:19:17.745
If you enjoyed this video, why
not click on the video above?

